# Growing blue crystals from copper sulfate
#
# Blue vitriol is the crystal every school grows: dissolve as much copper
# sulfate as hot water will take, let it cool, and the water hands most of
# it back as big blue crystals. The blue is not the copper alone — it is
# the five waters locked into the crystal with it, which is why the solid
# has its own name.
register lv2

# Thirty grams into a hundred millilitres is more than cold water can hold,
# so some of it comes straight back out as chalcanthite. Look at the liquid
# it leaves: so concentrated that the bench calls it BLACK rather than
# blue, which is what a strong copper sulfate solution really looks like in
# a beaker — the colour saturates and you cannot see through it.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 CuSO4 30g
look v1

# Heat it and the water takes the lot.
heat v1 20kJ
inspect v1

# Then cool it right down. This is the crystal-growing step, and the bench
# writes the equation for it: the copper, the sulfate and five waters going
# into one solid. And now the liquid reads BLUE rather than black — not
# because anything changed colour, but because so much of the copper has
# left it that you can see through what remains.
cool v1 30kJ
look v1
inspect v1

# What this does not model: the crystal. No shape, no faces, no single
# large specimen grown on a thread over a week — the bench says how much
# solid comes back and what colour it is, and a crystal-growing competition
# is judged on everything else.
#
# You will also see the bench decline three other copper solids that the
# database says the liquid is supersaturated against — Langite, Antlerite,
# Brochantite, the greens of weathered copper. They are named and refused
# rather than silently skipped, because a solution that would really throw
# them is not a solution this beaker is describing honestly.
